  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH THURSDAY/...\nIssued at 323 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nFor the extended period... guidance suggests upper level ridging may \nbe weaker than originally anticipated. This will allow for a gradual \nreturn to near daily thunderstorm chances beginning on Saturday \nevening now. There is even a potential on Friday night/early \nSaturday morning, but currently expecting the strongest storms to \nremain across the east and north. However, across north central \nNebraska, some minimal instability across the region could support \nsome daytime showers and thunderstorms on Saturday. A series of \ntroughs will continue to pivot through through Nebraska and the \ncentral Plains allowing for near daily afternoon/evening \nthunderstorms Wednesday. These storms will be primarily diurnally \ndriven and could intensify overnight as the low level jet increases. \nAt this time the severe risk remains uncertain, but would not be \nsurprised if a stronger storm or two develops with gusty winds being \nthe main threat, although some small hail could be possible in some \nof the stronger storms.  \n\nOtherwise, the only other major concern in the extended period will \nbe the increasing temperatures through Sunday. Highs will generally \nrise into the mid to upper 90s west of a Brady to Valentine line \nSaturday and Sunday. To the east of this line, highs are expected to \nrise into the low 90s. Warm temperatures continue into Monday, \nalthough, they won't be quite as high as the weekend as the overall \npattern slowly shifts and some cooler air will begin working into \nnorthern Nebraska. Expect a range from the upper 80s across north \ncentral to the upper 90s in southwest Nebraska on Monday. \nTemperatures fall back to near or slightly below normals on Tuesday \nand Wednesday ranging from the low 80s in north central to the low \n90s in southwest Nebraska.",
